{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016RT2aQDXtxAGFkbZ9EwTKg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment using Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/<name>/` - One directory per utility with main.go and tests\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System calls\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash utility shared library\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size parsing shared library\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utility shared library\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation\n\n**Current State**: All releases (00.0 through 15.0) have spec_complete status but code status is \"not started\" for everything. The repo was reset to specs-only on 2026-05-13. This is generation-run44, a fresh generation run.\n\n**Requirement States**: All requirements across all SRDs are in \"ready\" status.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nFollowing the release-driven priority, I'm starting with release 00.0, which contains the foundational infrastructure pieces: magefiles, format, sys, testutils, hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil. All seven components are currently at \"not started\" status.\n\n## Dependency Analysis\n\nLooking at the dependency graph, I need to sequence the work carefully—srd003-format depends on srd002-sys, and both are prerequisites for downstream packages like ls and du. The build order should start with testutils and sys as independent foundations, then format once sys is ready, followed by the utility packages, with magefiles last as build infrastructure. I'm also considering how to split implementation and tests according to the priority guidelines, though that constraint seems to be cut off mid-thought.\n\nNow I'm checking the contract requirements against what's already defined in the prompt—there's a `package_contracts` field that already specifies contracts for several packages, but the constraint says to propose contract tasks before implementation tasks for new packages. I need to clarify whether the existing contract definitions mean I should skip proposing separate contract tasks, or if I still need to create them as formal tasks in the proposal.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit, I should prioritize which packages to tackle first—the foundational ones with no inter-package dependencies like srd001-testutils and srd002-sys can start in parallel, while others that depend on them should wait for contract tasks to be completed first.\n\nNow I'm checking the weight budgets and ready requirements for srd001-testutils to see what can actually be tackled in the first batch. I'm realizing the ready requirements for srd001-testutils start at R3.3 and go through R5.2, which means the earlier requirement sections aren't in the ready list—they're either already completed or don't exist in this context. Since the code was reset but the requirement_states tracks what needs work, I need to figure out if those missing sections are actually done or just not applicable to this generation cycle.\n\nGiven the constraint to only propose tasks for requirements marked \"ready,\" I should stick to R3.3 through R5.2 and split them into groups of four, with the first task covering R3.3 through R3.6 at maximum weight.\n\nFor srd002-sys, I have four ready requirements that fit neatly into a single task. Now I'm working through srd003-format, which has a larger set of ready requirements—I need to count them carefully to determine how many tasks are needed and how to distribute them.\n\nGiven the hard constraint of only four tasks total, I need to be strategic about which SRDs to prioritize. The contract tasks are essential since they define the Go stub files, even though the package_contracts section already specifies what those contracts should be. I'm thinking through whether I can combine some contracts to stay within the limit, but each task can only handle up to four sub-requirements.\n\nLet me focus on what would be most valuable: starting with testutils and sys since other components depend on them. I could batch the contract tasks together—propose stubs for testutils, sys, sizeparse, and hashutil all at once, then handle implementations in the next batch. But that feels inefficient since the contracts are already defined in the package specs and would be quick to write. I think the better approach is alternating contracts and implementations: contract for testutils, contract for sys, then their implementations, which gets me two packages partially done in this batch. maybe 20-50 lines each. The constraint says \"Target 300-500 lines per task\" for code tasks. These would be well under that.\n\nBut the constraints also say \"Combine trivial changes into one task\" (P2: \"Combine trivial changes into one task\").\n\nHmm, but each package is a separate directory. Let me think...\n\nActually, let me re-read the contracts constraint more carefully:\n\n\"Before proposing implementation tasks for a new package, propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs. Title it with the suffix '(contract)' and set its dependency to -1. Implementation tasks for that package must list the contract file in required_reading and depend on the contract task. Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\"\n\nSo the contracts must come before implementations. Given 4-task limit, I think the most efficient approach is to do 2 contracts plus 2 implementations for the most critical packages, which makes progress while respecting the dependency chain. Let me structure it as: srd002-sys contract first since it's foundational, then srd001-testutils contract, followed by their implementations that depend on those contracts. But wait, I need to think about what requirements a contract task actually covers—it's not tied to specific requirements the way implementation tasks are. I'm listing out the contract and implementation tasks for both packages, with the contracts as prerequisites since they define all the type stubs. But I realize I need to verify what requirements R3.3 through R3.6 actually cover for testutils—I only have the requirement states showing readiness, not the full SRD content itself.\n\nThe SRD files live in `docs/specs/software-requirements/` and the stitch agent will need to read those to understand the actual requirement details. For the contract tasks, I'm wrestling with how to anchor them to specific SRD requirements when they're really creating the entire interface at once rather than implementing individual requirements. The constraint requires anchoring every task to SRD requirement IDs, so I need to figure out how to map the contract creation to those requirements even though it's a single task that produces all the stubs. I'm settling on a clearer approach: contract tasks are infrastructure that define the full public API as stubs upfront, while implementation tasks fill in the actual logic for ready requirements. This separates concerns nicely—the contract establishes the shape, and implementations follow. I'll structure the four tasks around dependencies, with testutils and sys as foundations that everything else builds on. I'm working through how to anchor contract tasks to requirements—they define type stubs rather than implementing behavior, so they don't map directly to SRD sub-requirements like implementation tasks do. I think the pragmatic approach is to reference the SRD generally for contract tasks and let implementation tasks handle the specific R-items, keeping in mind that the weight budget constraint applies to SRD sub-requirements included in a task, not to the contract definitions themselves.\n\nNow I'm looking at the srd002-sys package structure—it exports stat-related functions like FileInfo and Stat, terminal utilities like TerminalWidth and IsTerminal, and signal handling like InstallSIGPIPEHandler. These concerns should probably be split across multiple source files following Go conventions, so I need to figure out the right file organization based on what's already established. IsTerminal, OnTerminalResize) and pkg/sys/signal.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler). For srd001-testutils, I'd put DiffTest, NormalizeFunc, RunDiffTests, ComposeNormalizers, and TimestampNormalizer in pkg/testutils/difftest.go, following the constraint that the primary file must be named after the primary type. Now I need to think through what tasks would actually move things forward—looking at the dependency graph, srd001-testutils has no package dependencies but every command needs it for testing, and srd002-sys is similarly foundational for srd003-format and other packages downstream.\n\nSince I can propose 4 tasks and contracts must come before implementations, I should prioritize the most critical packages: sys and testutils. But that leaves me with 4 tasks just for contracts and implementations of 2 packages. Actually, I'm wondering if I should combine some of the smaller contracts—sizeparse and encutil are both pretty minimal in scope, so maybe I could batch those together and free up a task slot for something else. to't have the actual SRD content to reference - I only know the requirement IDs and their states, so I'll need to tell the stitch agent to read the SRD files directly and implement those specific requirements. For the contract tasks, I should describe what mechanical work they involve: creating Go files with package declarations, imports, type definitions, function signatures, and variable declarations based on the package_contracts section. I'm realizing the tension here—contract tasks need to define the entire public API surface, not just implement specific requirements. So rather than mapping to individual SRD items, the contract task should focus on cross-cutting concerns: defining all exported types and function signatures as stubs, then ensuring the package compiles. This is about shaping the API, not implementing behavior.\n\nFor the implementation tasks, I need to be practical. Looking at the ready requirements for srd002-sys—R2.6, R3.1, R3.2, R3.3—I don't have the actual SRD content, so I'll need to trust the stitch agent to read those requirements and implement them. The task description should reference the SRD file and list which requirements to implement, but I'm concerned the task needs to be self-contained enough that the agent understands what it's actually building without having to hunt down the SRD separately.\n\nLet me structure this with two tasks: one for the contract definitions that lists the function signatures and types explicitly, and another for the implementation that references the SRD requirements. That way the contract task is clear and self-contained, while the implementation task can point to the detailed behavior spec.\n\n**Task 0: pkg/sys (contract) — srd002-sys types and signatures**\n- Creates stub files for pkg/sys package\n- dependency: -1\n- Files: pkg/sys/stat.go, pkg/sys/terminal.go, pkg/sys/signal.go\n- Required reading: docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml\n- Requirements:\n  - R1: Define FileInfo struct with all fields per srd002-sys\n  - R2: Define Stat and Lstat function signatures\n  - R3: Define TerminalWidth, IsTerminal, OnTerminalResize function signatures\n  - R4: Define InstallSIGPIPEHandler function signature\n\nThat gives me 4 requirements at weight 1 each, totaling weight 4.\n\n**Task 1: pkg/testutils (contract) — srd001-testutils types and signatures**\n- Creates stub file for pkg/testutils package\n- dependency: -1\n- Files: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n- Required reading: docs/specs/software-requirements/srd001-testutils.yaml\n- Requirements:\n  - R1: Define DiffTest struct with all fields per srd001-testutils\n  - R2: Define NormalizeFunc type and RunDiffTests function signature\n  - R3: Define ComposeNormalizers function signature and TimestampNormalizer variable\n\nThat's 3 requirements at weight 1 for a total of 3.\n\n**Task 2: pkg/sys implementation — srd002-sys R2.6, R3.1, R3.2, R3.3**\n- Implements the ready requirements for pkg/sys\n- dependency: 0\n- Files: pkg/sys/stat.go, pkg/sys/terminal.go, pkg/sys/signal.go\n- Required reading: docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml\n- Requirements reference the specific SRD sections for detailed behavior\n\n**Task 3: pkg/testutils implementation — srd001-testutils R3.3, R3.4, R3.5, R3.6**\n- Implements some ready requirements for pkg/testutils\n- dependency: 1\n- Files: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n- Required reading: docs/specs/software-requirements/srd001-testutils.yaml\n\nWeight check: 4 requirements × weight 1 = 4, which fits within the limit.\n\nI need to clarify the requirement namespaces here — the task-level requirements (R1, R2, R3) are distinct from the SRD requirement IDs (like srd002-sys R2.6), and each task maps to its corresponding SRD items. For pkg/sys, the external dependency on golang.org/x/sys/unix needs to already be in go.mod, while pkg/testutils only relies on standard library packages like testing, os, and os/exec.\n\nI'm also verifying that the constraint about maximum 4 SRD sub-requirements per task is satisfied — I'm proposing exactly 4 for each implementation task, which hits the limit. The go.mod file should already exist per the project constraints, so the stitch agent can assume it's there with the necessary dependencies included.\n\nNow I need to figure out the contract file structure for the sys package, since it encompasses multiple concerns like file stats, terminal operations, and signal handling. I should map out which functions belong in the contract definition before finalizing the task breakdown.\n\nFor testutils, the primary export is DiffTest, so that'll be straightforward to organize into a single file. For the actual implementation, I'll use panic stubs since that's the standard approach for compilable Go contract stubs—functions need proper return statements, and structs need all their fields defined.\n\nMy plan is to create two contract tasks: one for sys covering the stat, terminal, and signal interfaces, and one for testutils with the DiffTest type.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the implementation tasks. I need to be careful about how I organize the sys package files—looking back at the exports, I see FileInfo, Stat, Lstat, TerminalWidth, and IsTerminal, which suggests I should split the implementation across stat.go, terminal.go, and signal.go based on their functionality. I'm realizing the contract just needs stubs, so I should keep the file structure simpler—maybe consolidating into fewer files rather than splitting across stat.go, terminal.go, and signal.go. Let me reconsider whether fileinfo.go makes sense as the primary file given the package's multiple concerns. I'm noticing a discrepancy between the CLAUDE.md examples that reference BuildBinary and the actual package contract for srd001-testutils, which doesn't list it. I need to stick with what's in the contract since that's the authoritative source, unless BuildBinary is covered by a non-ready requirement I'm missing.\n\nActually, I should trust the process here—the SRD contains all the details, and the stitch agent will read it to understand what each requirement means. For the implementation tasks, I can just reference the specific R-items like \"Implement srd002-sys R2.6\" and let the agent figure out the details from the SRD itself, rather than trying to spell everything out in the task requirements. I