efore implementations, and checking that tie-breaking by alphabetical file path puts sys before testutils. Now I need to write detailed descriptions for the stitch agent, starting with what the format contract needs to export: the HumanSizeOpts struct, HumanSize function, and File type.\n\nThe package also exports color-related functions like TypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled and its setters, plus padding and column utilities. It imports from io, os, and the sys package, but can't depend on anything in cmd/. Since this package handles multiple concerns—size formatting, color output, text layout—I'll organize the source files by concern: one for human-readable size formatting, another for color operations, and so on.\n\nLooking at the contract requirements, the primary exported type is HumanSizeOpts which pairs with HumanSize, so that goes in humansize.go. The color functions belong together in color.go, and the padding and column functions go in column.go. For the srd002-sys implementation, I need to tell the stitch agent which package and files to modify.\n\nFor the contract task, I should title it with the \"(contract)\" suffix and set dependency to -1. For implementation tasks, I'll include the specific SRD requirement IDs in the title. I'm also considering whether to propose testutils tasks—with 10 ready requirements available, I could split them into smaller batches like R3.3-R3.6 for the first task.\n\nGiven my 4-task limit, I'm weighing whether to allocate them as: 1 format contract, 1 sys implementation, and then either 2 testutils tasks or 1 testutils plus 1 format implementation. The second approach advances more packages but defers some testutils work.\n\nThe real constraint is that format implementation depends on both the format contract and the system implementation being complete, but my dependency model only supports a single parent task. I need to figure out how to handle this multi-dependency situation within the 4-task structure. exists from earlier.